{"causes":["Jules Verne's early fascination with geology and engineering led him to create a narrative that would explore the intersection of science and adventure.","The scientific community was increasingly interested in exploring the Earth's crust during the mid-19th century, providing a fertile ground for Verne's ideas to take root.","The publication of Verne's earlier work, 'Five Weeks in a Balloon' (1863), demonstrated his ability to craft compelling stories that combined science and excitement, paving the way for 'A Journey to the Centre of the Earth'."],"effects":["A Journey to the Centre of the Earth helped establish Jules Verne as one of the leading figures in the development of science fiction.","The novel's popularity contributed to a renewed public interest in geology and natural sciences, inspiring new generations of scientists and explorers.","Verne's work influenced the creation of numerous scientific expeditions and explorations, including the drilling of the first deep well in Germany in 1866.","A Journey to the Centre of the Earth has been adapted into numerous films, stage productions, and other works of fiction, cementing its place in popular culture.","The novel's themes of adventure, exploration, and scientific discovery have become staples of science fiction literature, inspiring countless authors and creators."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"jules-verne","sourceName":"Jules Verne","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"a-journey-to-the-centre-of-the-earth","targetName":"A Journey to the Centre of the Earth","context":"Verne's novel was a direct result of his scientific interests and earlier literary endeavors."},{"sourceSlug":"geology","sourceName":"Geology","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-journey-to-the-centre-of-the-earth","targetName":"A Journey to the Centre of the Earth","context":"The scientific community's growing interest in geology during the mid-19th century laid the groundwork for Verne's ideas."},{"sourceSlug":"science-fiction","sourceName":"Science Fiction","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-journey-to-the-centre-of-the-earth","targetName":"A Journey to the Centre of the Earth","context":"Verne's work helped establish science fiction as a distinct genre, paving the way for future authors and creators."},{"sourceSlug":"germany","sourceName":"Germany","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-journey-to-the-centre-of-the-earth","targetName":"A Journey to the Centre of the Earth","context":"The drilling of the first deep well in Germany in 1866 was inspired by Verne's novel."},{"sourceSlug":"gutenberg","sourceName":"Gutenberg","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-journey-to-the-centre-of-the-earth","targetName":"A Journey to the Centre of the Earth","context":"The novel was first published in 1864 by Jules Verne's publisher, Adolphe Lévy."},{"sourceSlug":"adolphe-levy","sourceName":"Adolphe Lévy","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-journey-to-the-centre-of-the-earth","targetName":"A Journey to the Centre of the Earth","context":"Lévy played a crucial role in promoting Verne's work, including 'A Journey to the Centre of the Earth'."},{"sourceSlug":"john-hyde-paulding","sourceName":"John Hyde Paulding","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-journey-to-the-centre-of-the-earth","targetName":"A Journey to the Centre of the Earth","context":"Paulding's own science fiction work was influenced by Verne's novel."},{"sourceSlug":"edgar-allan-poe","sourceName":"Edgar Allan Poe","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-journey-to-the-centre-of-the-earth","targetName":"A Journey to the Centre of the Earth","context":"Poe's own science fiction work was influenced by Verne's novel."},{"sourceSlug":"nathaniel-hawthorne","sourceName":"Nathaniel Hawthorne","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-journey-to-the-centre-of-the-earth","targetName":"A Journey to the Centre of the Earth","context":"Hawthorne's own science fiction work was influenced by Verne's novel."},{"sourceSlug":"mark-twain","sourceName":"Mark Twain","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-journey-to-the-centre-of-the-earth","targetName":"A Journey to the Centre of the Earth","context":"Twain's own science fiction work was influenced by Verne's novel."}],"places":[{"name":"Berlin, Germany","role":"Publication place"},{"name":"Paris, France","role":"Place of inspiration"},{"name":"Strasbourg, Alsace","role":"Setting for key events"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: A Journey to the Centre of the Earth","type":"Reference article","slug":"a-journey-to-the-centre-of-the-earth"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q219457","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Journey_to_the_Centre_of_the_Earth"],"tags":["literary work","Novels & Prose Fiction","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"A Journey to the Centre of the Earth contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"A Journey to the Centre of the Earth (French: Voyage au centre de la Terre) is the science fiction adventure novel by Jules Verne (1828–1905), published in 1864 (serialised in Pierre-Jules Hetzel's Magazine d'éducation et de récréation, then as a book in November 1864) — the third of Verne's extraordinary 'Voyages extraordinaires' series, which would eventually comprise 54 novels and establish Verne as the founding figure of modern science fiction. The novel follows the expedition of Professor Otto Lindenbrock (an eccentric German geologist), his nephew Axel (the narrator), and the Icelandic guide Hans, who descend through a volcanic crater in Iceland (the Snæfellsjökull) following a coded message left by the 16th-century Icelandic scientist Arne Saknussemm, descending to discover an enormous underground ocean, prehistoric creatures (plesiosaurs and ichthyosaurs, mastodons), an electrical storm, and a prehistoric human ('the Guardian') tending a herd of mastodons, before being ejected to the surface by a volcanic eruption through the crater of Stromboli in Sicily.

A Journey to the Centre of the Earth exemplifies Verne's characteristic method: the meticulous, scientifically informed extrapolation from contemporary knowledge to an imagined but plausible scenario — the underground geology of the novel is based on the actual scientific debates of the 1860s about the interior of the earth (whether it was molten or solid), and Verne fills his underground world with the actual prehistoric creatures being discovered in the fossil record by the geologists and palaeontologists of his era. Lindenbrock's debate with the prevailing theory (that the earth's interior is at high temperature) against a competing hypothesis (that it might remain solid and at lower temperatures) is grounded in the actual scientific controversy, and Verne's novel takes the minority position that allows his hero to survive.

Verne's 'Voyages extraordinaires' — comprising A Journey to the Centre of the Earth, Twenty Thousand Leagues Under the Sea (1870), Around the World in Eighty Days (1872), and 51 further novels — are the founding texts of modern science fiction as a genre, establishing its characteristic combination of scientific extrapolation, adventure narrative, and educational purpose ('to outline all the geographical, geological, physical, and astronomical knowledge amassed by modern science and to rewrite the history of the universe').","subjects":["Science Fiction","Classical Era","Jules Verne","French Literature","19th Century","Adventure","Geology","Victorian Era"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"A Journey to the Centre of the Earth has had a lasting impact on the science fiction genre, inspiring countless authors and creators while also influencing real-world scientific inquiry.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
